+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static marketing site for The Daily Grind Coffee Co. Served as plain files
+(`pnpm start` runs a static file server); there is no build step.
+
+## Encrypted delivery notes
+
+The homepage lets customers attach private notes for the courier (gate codes,
+allergies, drop-off instructions). Notes are encrypted **in the browser** before
+anything is sent, so the web server, database and anyone with read access to
+them only ever see ciphertext. Only the kitchen's secret key can open a note.
+
+### Cryptography
+
+Suite identifier: `XWING-HKDF-SHA256-AES256GCM`
+
+| Step | Primitive | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Shared secret | **X-Wing** hybrid KEM = ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203, post-quantum) + X25519 | `draft-connolly-cfrg-xwing-kem`; an attacker must break both components. Implementation: vendored, audited `@noble/post-quantum`. |
+| Key derivation | HKDF-SHA-256 (WebCrypto) | `info` = label ‖ SHA-256(envelope header); fresh key per note, non-extractable. |
+| Encryption | AES-256-GCM (WebCrypto) | 96-bit random nonce, 128-bit tag, envelope header as AAD. |
+| Padding | 2-byte length prefix, zero-padded to 128-byte blocks | Hides note length. |
+
+The envelope header (version, suite, key id, purpose, order reference,
+timestamp and the KEM ciphertext) is bound into both the derived key and the
+GCM tag, so changing any of it makes decryption fail.
+
+**Not provided:** sender authentication. X-Wing is a KEM, so an envelope proves
+it was encrypted to the store key, not who encrypted it. The logged-in ordering
+session that submits the envelope is responsible for customer identity.
+
+### Files
+
+| Path | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `js/delivery-notes-crypto.js` | Shared browser/Node module: key handling, `encryptDeliveryNote`, `decryptDeliveryNote`, envelope parsing. |
+| `js/delivery-notes.js` | Homepage UI. Fetches the store key, encrypts, keeps the envelope (never the plaintext) in `localStorage` for the next order. |
+| `vendor/noble-xwing.js` | Bundled `@noble/post-quantum` 0.7.1 X-Wing KEM. Rebuild with `scripts/build-vendor.sh`. |
+| `keys/delivery-recipient.json` | Published store public key (`kid`, suite, base64 key, expiry). Safe to commit. |
+| `keys/delivery-recipient.secret.json` | Store secret seed. **Git-ignored**; lives only on the kitchen/back-office system. |
+| `scripts/generate-recipient-keys.mjs` | Creates/rotates the key pair (`pnpm keys:generate [kid] [--force]`). |
+| `scripts/decrypt-note.mjs` | Kitchen-side decrypt: `pnpm notes:decrypt envelope.json [secret.json]` (use `-` for stdin). |
+| `test/delivery-notes-crypto.test.mjs` | Round-trip, tamper, wrong-key, padding and validation tests (`pnpm test`, Node ≥ 20). |
+
+### Envelope format
+
+```json
+{
+  "v": 1,
+  "suite": "XWING-HKDF-SHA256-AES256GCM",
+  "kid": "dg-delivery-2026-10",
+  "ctx": { "purpose": "delivery-note", "orderRef": "DG-10422", "createdAt": "2026-10-28T02:11:14.103Z" },
+  "kemCt": "<base64, 1120 bytes>",
+  "nonce": "<base64, 12 bytes>",
+  "ct": "<base64, padded plaintext + 16-byte tag>"
+}
+```
+
+### Key rotation
+
+1. On the kitchen system run `pnpm keys:generate dg-delivery-YYYY-MM --force`.
+2. Commit the new `keys/delivery-recipient.json`; keep the `.secret.json` file private.
+3. Keep the previous secret until all envelopes with the old `kid` have been read.
+4. Print the fingerprint shown by the generator on receipts so customers can
+   compare it with the one displayed on the homepage.
